# Automatic Nomor RM and required patient fields (rule 244)

Source: the internal memo of 2026-10-17 (M4.1, M4.2) and the owner's written decisions of 2026-11-17, recorded in
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-comprehensive-closure-1.md` §2c; the plan, impact analysis and compatibility plan are in
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-comprehensive-closure-1-memo-implementation-plan.md`. Delivered in PRs; each PR adds its section.

## 0. Owner decisions (CG-22)

- M4.1 "Auto, read-only": the system generates the Nomor RM; Front Office cannot edit it; the DaengtisiaMS format
  `DG-{KODE_CABANG}-{TAHUN}-{NOMOR}` is kept; locking and database uniqueness; existing numbers never changed; Legacy
  RME compatibility kept; CC1-PAT-1 measured read-only on production before the automatic number is switched on; safe
  overflow handling; the Sprint 23.8 manual-number rule revised explicitly (this rule).
- Plan defaults accepted (2026-11-17): (1) an old paper-card number only through Supervisor RME, with a reason,
  audited; (2) the no-phone exception through Front Office with a reason code, audited and reportable; (3) after
  unusually high existing numbers the sequence continues after the highest — a number is never reused; (4) the legacy
  patient CSV import commit needs the paper-card permission (staging stays with Front Office; revises part of rule 168);
  (5) no correction tool for existing numbers in this sprint — number, branch and registration date are frozen on edit.

## 1. The counter and the allocator (PR-1)

1. **The counter.** `mst_patient_rm_sequences` holds one row per branch and year (`UNIQUE(branch_id, year)`):
   `last_number` is the highest sequence issued or seeded. Additive migration only (CG-09).
2. **The allocator.** `PatientRmNumberAllocator::allocate(Branch, int $year)` is the only issuer:
   - it refuses to run outside the caller's open transaction (the registration transaction), so the row lock lasts
     until the patient row commits;
   - it refuses MAIN (never an RME branch) and an unknown branch code;
   - it locks the branch-year row FOR UPDATE BEFORE reading the counter (`lockOrCreate`: a missing row is created with
     INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, seeded from the highest issued number, then locked), so two registrations in one
     branch-year serialise and the second reads the first's committed value;
   - it skips a candidate while it exists in ANY spelling (`PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::numericSpellings`: the
     canonical code and its historical aliases from `BranchCodeAlias` — TKM1/TLK1, SUN4/SPN4 — times every zero padding
     up to nine digits) on any patient, soft-deleted ones included (rule 169: they reserve their number), or as an
     active merge alias (`PatientRmAliasRepositoryInterface::findActiveByNumbers`);
   - it composes with four-digit padding (`formatSequence`), widens past 9999 (10000, …) rather than stopping
     registration, logs a capacity warning from 9000 with branch id, year and digit count only (never a number), and
     refuses once a sequence would need nine digits (`PatientRmNumberUnavailableException`, an operator message
     without a number);
   - before the counter moves, the composed number must round-trip through `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::parse()`
     to the same branch code and sequence; a branch code the format cannot carry (a '-' in it, more than 16 characters)
     is refused with the same exception, never issued as an unparseable number.
3. **Never rewritten.** Nothing here updates `mst_patients`. `compose()` and `parse()` are unchanged; numbers issued
   before this rule, in any shape, stay as they are.
4. **Seeding.** `php artisan patient:rm-sequence-seed` (runtime user; refuses root, and fails closed — refuses — when
   the effective user cannot be determined, e.g. no posix extension): dry run by default — per branch-year counts
   (numbers, non-numeric sequences, above 9999, ignored as oversized, ignored above `--ignore-above`) and the proposed
   counter's DIGIT COUNT only (`PROPOSED_COUNTER_DIGITS`), totals for MAIN numbers, unknown codes and non-canonical
   values; never a Nomor RM, never the counter value (printed beside the branch and year it would complete a real
   patient's number; the repository is public, CG-11), never a patient detail. An existing sequence wider than the
   allocator issues (more than `PatientRmNumberAllocator::MAX_DIGITS` digits, e.g. a phone number typed as the
   sequence) can never collide with an issuable number, so the lazy seed and the plan both ignore it (counted as
   `IGNORED_OVERSIZED`): seeded, it would push the counter past the cap and, since a counter is never lowered, stop
   automatic numbering for that branch-year for good. `--apply` raises each counter
   to GREATEST(current, proposed) — never lower, idempotent — and writes one `PATIENT_RM_SEQUENCE_SEEDED` audit row of
   counts. Scope: the current clinic year (`ClinicalClock`) unless `--all-years`; `--ignore-above=N` keeps unusually
   high numbers out of the counter (they stay reserved: the allocator still skips them). Running `--apply` on
   production is a separate owner-approved step. The allocator seeds a missing branch-year on first use itself, so
   correctness never depends on the command.
5. **Lock order.** Counter row → patient insert → (later PRs) the visit's own locks. Since PR-2 the counter row is
   locked only through `PatientRmNumberAllocator`: `allocate()` (registration), `claimExisting()` (a typed number:
   the legacy CSV commit, PR-3's paper card and pilot importer) and `lockCounter()` (the CSV commit's sorted pre-lock);
   the seed command raises it with `raiseTo()`. No other code locks or writes it.
6. **Dormant (PR-1 only).** In PR-1 no registration path called the allocator. PR-2 switched NEW registrations to it
   (§2); a typed number survives only for a number that already exists outside the system (§2.6).

   **PR-2 must** (done — §2.5, §2.6) close the other Nomor RM writers (recorded by the PR-1 independent review, not implemented in PR-1):
   the legacy patient CSV import validation (`LegacyPatientImportService::takenValues`) and commit (its
   `lockForUpdate()->exists()` check), the explicit-number path in `PatientService` (an explicit `medical_record_number`
   kept verbatim) and `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::exists()` each check only the exact spelling and never take
   the branch-year counter lock. PR-2 routes every one of them through `numericSpellings` (every alias code and zero
   padding) plus the active merge aliases, under the branch-year counter lock (lock order of item 5), and compares
   case-insensitively (as `findActiveByNumbers` already does), so a manual or imported number can never take, or
   duplicate in another spelling, a number the allocator issues or will issue.
7. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/RME/FrontOfficePatientRmNumberAllocatorTest.php` (AL-01..AL-15) and
   `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficePatientRmSequenceSeedCommandTest.php` (SD-01..SD-10) — token `FrontOffice`;
   `tests/Feature/RME/ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrencyPatientRmSequenceTest.php` (PostgreSQL; committed writer + NOWAIT
   probe + `lock_timeout`; it pins that the counter row is locked when it is READ, not only when it is written) — token
   `ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrency`. All three are declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

## 2. The automatic number at registration (PR-2, active)

1. **One issuer for new patients.** `PatientService::create()` is the only path that registers a patient (Master Data →
   Tambah Pasien and Kunjungan → Pasien Baru through `ClinicVisitService`). Inside its transaction it resolves the
   branch (required — `branch_id` on the master form, the visit's branch on Pasien Baru; MAIN, an inactive or a
   non-RME branch is refused), stamps `registered_at` with `ClinicalClock::today()` and takes the number from
   `PatientRmNumberAllocator::allocate($branch, clinical year)`. A posted `medical_record_number`, `manual_rm_number`
   or `registered_at` is dropped by the service (and no longer validated by `StorePatientRequest` or
   `StoreClinicVisitRequest`); the request-time "Nomor RM final … sudah digunakan" pre-checks are gone because there is
   nothing to pre-check. 00:30 WITA on 1 January belongs to the new year although UTC is still 31 December.
   **Branch scope (PR-2 review F2, CG-05; CC1-PAT-2 store path).** The branch must be one the actor is working
   (`RmeWorkingBranchScope::allows()`): a context-bound desk account (Front Office, Admin Klinik, Kasir, Perawat) may
   register only into its working branch — the request may narrow, never widen. Master Data: the form offers only
   the allowed branches, `StorePatientRequest` answers **422** (`branch_id.in`) for another one (a stale or edited
   form is a user-correctable input error), and `PatientService::create($data, $actor)` answers **403** to any caller
   that still names one (an authorization breach, as `ClinicVisitService::resolveBranchId()` does). Kunjungan → Pasien
   Baru: `StoreClinicVisitRequest` pins the pinned desk account's new patient to its working branch and
   `ClinicVisitService::resolveBranchId()` re-asserts the scope (403); it passes no actor to `create()`, whose null
   actor means "every active RME branch" (passing it would add `ClinicVisitService` to this PR — recorded).
   **Every writer is governed except the pilot backup importer** (`rme:import-pilot-backup`), closed in PR-3.
2. **Retired.** The free override (a full `medical_record_number` typed on the master form) and the UTC fallback
   generator (`PatientCodeGenerator`, a CG-12 deviation; deleted with `PatientCodeGenerationTest`, and the
   `config/patient.php` block emptied) are gone. Existing rows
   keep whatever number they hold.
3. **Frozen on edit (default 5).** `PatientService::update()` drops `medical_record_number`, `manual_rm_number`,
   `branch_id` and `registered_at`; `UpdatePatientRequest` no longer accepts them; the edit form shows them read-only.
   There is no correction tool for existing numbers.
4. **Unique violations.** The insert runs in a savepoint inside the registration transaction, so the counter row lock
   taken by the allocator stays held across retries. A unique violation on `medical_record_number` (a writer that did
   not take the counter lock, e.g. an old PHP process during a rolling deploy) rolls back the insert only, the
   allocator issues the next number (the collided one is genuinely taken), and this is retried up to
   `PatientService::UNIQUE_RETRIES` (3) times — one attempt plus three retries — then a 422 on `medical_record_number`
   and the whole transaction rolls back (counter included). A `ktp_number` violation answers 422 on `ktp_number` at
   once. Any other unique violation is rethrown untouched (review F4). No raw SQL or number in a message.
   `PatientRmNumberUnavailableException` (MAIN, unknown or unparseable code, the 8-digit cap) answers 422 on
   `medical_record_number` with its operator message.
5. **One uniqueness question, in every spelling.** `PatientRmNumberAllocator::isTaken()` is the single answer to "is this
   Nomor RM taken?": `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::collisionSpellings()` (the value as given plus, once
   uppercased, every equivalent branch code × every zero padding up to 18 digits for a numeric sequence, or every
   equivalent code for a non-numeric one) checked against every patient, soft-deleted ones included, and every active
   merge alias. **Patients are compared by numeric VALUE in any padding (review F1):**
   `PatientRmSequenceRepository::anyNumberIssued()` matches a canonical all-digit number by its lowered prefix
   (`dg-{code}-{year}-`, every equivalent code) and `LTRIM(SUBSTR(...), '0')` = the value, so `…-000000000006` and a
   30-digit padding are the same number as `…-6`; `numericValueOf()` / `collisionKey()` apply the same rule in PHP
   (`takenAmong()`, the allocator's skip), so all three agree. Merge aliases are compared exactly against the spellings
   (an alias is a copy of its source patient's number, which the value comparison already covers). `PatientMedicalRecordNumberService::exists()` delegates to it; `collisionKey()` gives one comparison
   key per identity (in-file duplicates in the legacy CSV); `takenAmong()` answers a whole batch in a bounded number of
   queries (one read per branch-year, one alias query per chunk of 50, one more per alias found).
6. **Numbers that already exist (legacy compatibility).** A number that comes from outside the system — the legacy
   patient CSV import now, the Supervisor RME paper-card exception and the pilot backup importer in PR-3 — is stored
   verbatim, never renumbered, and only after `PatientRmNumberAllocator::claimExisting($branch, $number)` has locked
   that number's branch-year counter row (lazily creating it, seeded like the allocator) and found no spelling taken.
   **A successful claim raises the counter** to the claimed value — GREATEST, never lower, under the same lock — when
   that value has at most `MAX_DIGITS` (8) digits (the seed's oversized filter; a wider value is only reserved), so the
   sequence continues after the highest number on record (owner default 3, followed literally). The allocator's
   skip stays as the safety net and finds the first free value with ONE set-based read of the branch-year
   (`firstFreeAbove()`), not a per-number loop. `LegacyPatientImportService` refuses at staging a manual sequence
   longer than 18 digits, or wider than 9 characters whose value fits 8 digits once leading zeros are stripped (a
   second spelling of an issuable number); stages and revalidates through `isTaken()`/`takenAmong()` and
   value-aware in-file keys; and its commit locks every branch-year counter the batch touches, sorted by branch then
   year, before the first insert, then claims each row with `claimExisting()` (review F6: one code path; it sees the
   batch's own earlier inserts and raises the counter). A claim far above the counter is not flagged at staging
   (optional review item, not built). Archive branch derivation and alias folding are unchanged.
7. **Lock order and namespace.** Counter row (`mst_patient_rm_sequences`, `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`) → patient insert →
   the visit's own locks (`lockVisitSequence`, advisory 92_001, and later ones). The counter is a ROW lock, not an
   advisory lock, so it takes no number in the 91_xxx/92_xxx advisory inventory on
   `ClinicVisitRepository::PATIENT_ENCOUNTER_LOCK_NAMESPACE`. The legacy commit takes its batch header and staging-row
   locks first, then the counters in sorted order, so two imports cannot deadlock on counters. Expression indexes on
   `LOWER(medical_record_number) text_pattern_ops` (mst_patients) and `LOWER(alias_medical_record_number)` serve the
   case-insensitive comparisons (additive migration `2026_11_18_100001`, PostgreSQL only, CONCURRENTLY).
8. **Views.** Both forms show "Nomor RM: otomatis — DG-{KODE}-{TAHUN}-####" with the clinical year (and the selected
   branch code once chosen); no number is previewed, because two operators would see the same one. The master form's
   success message carries the issued number; Pasien Baru shows it on the visit.
9. **Deploy.** `patient:rm-sequence-seed --apply` is NOT a prerequisite: the allocator seeds a missing branch-year from
   the highest number already issued (any spelling) on first use, under the same lock. Running the seed first only
   makes the counters visible in advance and lets `--ignore-above` keep an unusually high number out of a counter.
   Deploy outside clinic hours: a request served by a pre-deploy PHP process composes typed numbers without the lock
   (item 4 covers the collision). The additive index migration (item 7) runs with the deploy's
   `php artisan migrate --force`. Rollback is a code revert; the counter table and the indexes become inert.
10. **Tests.** `tests/Feature/Patient/FrontOfficePatientRmAutoNumberTest.php` (AN-01..AN-26) — token `FrontOffice`;
    `tests/Feature/Patient/LegacyPatientRmAutoNumberCompatibilityTest.php` (LP-01..LP-13) — token `LegacyPatient`;
    `tests/Feature/RME/ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrencyPatientRmSequenceTest.php` (PostgreSQL, two connections in one
    process: the full registration, the import claim and the REAL `LegacyPatientImportService::commit()` queuing behind
    an in-flight registration) — token `ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrency`. AN-22..AN-26 and LP-09..LP-13 cover the
    review fixes. All declared in
    `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

## 3. Sprint 23.8 revised explicitly

Sprint 23 Phase 23.8 (`docs/sprint_23_phase_23_8_patient_id_registration_flow.md`, the
`PatientMedicalRecordNumberService` docblock) said: the RM sequence is typed by the admin, "NOT auto-generated and NOT
auto-padded", and the composed value is checked for uniqueness by exact match. Rule 244 revises it:

- **Kept:** the format `DG-{KODE_CABANG}-{TAHUN}-{NOMOR}`, `compose()`/`parse()`, global uniqueness (now in every
  spelling), the clinical calendar owning the year (rule 110 §4), and never renumbering an existing patient.
- **Replaced for new registrations:** the sequence is issued by the allocator, padded to four digits, widened past
  9999; the registration date is the server's clinical day; the admin types neither.
- **Narrowed:** a typed sequence survives only for a number that already exists outside the system (legacy CSV import;
  PR-3 paper card), claimed under the counter lock.
- Rule 110 §4's list of request-time pre-check sites (`StorePatientRequest`, `UpdatePatientRequest`,
  `StoreClinicVisitRequest`) no longer applies: those pre-checks were removed with the typed number.
